title: Kyverno policies, hands-on
guide: howto/kyverno.html
promise: Learn the current policy types with the free kyverno command-line tool, with no cluster, then learn to read the older ClusterPolicy that existing clusters still run.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is intermediate and assumes Kubernetes basics: a Pod is one or more containers run together, and a Deployment keeps a set of identical Pods running. Allow about two hours.

You need the kyverno command-line interface (CLI), but no cluster.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Every command, policy and test ran as printed with Kyverno CLI 1.19.2.

No cluster was used, so cluster behavior comes from Kyverno's documentation, Helm chart and source code. Not run: step 11's workflow on GitHub, and the final 1.20.0 release.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Most steps judge step 2's sample files against a policy with the kyverno apply command. Paste BASH blocks into your terminal in ~/kyverno-lab. Save YAML blocks there, named by their first-line comment. Unlabelled blocks are output.

== Set up your environment
section: 1. Install the CLI and make a lab
On macOS or Linux, these lines install the CLI with Homebrew and make ~/kyverno-lab. Expect Version: 1.19.2 or later in 1.19; 1.20's release candidate agrees on steps 2 to 11 but refuses step 12's legacy policy. Get 1.19.2 from github.com/kyverno/kyverno/releases.
```bash
brew install kyverno
kyverno version
mkdir -p ~/kyverno-lab/policies && cd ~/kyverno-lab
```

== What Kyverno is and where you meet it
section: Policies that are Kubernetes resources
Kyverno checks requests to a Kubernetes cluster against policies you kubectl apply. Its current types use CEL (Common Expression Language): ValidatingPolicy checks a request, MutatingPolicy changes it, GeneratingPolicy and DeletingPolicy create and delete resources, and ImageValidatingPolicy checks image signatures.

== The one idea: the check comes last in a chain
section: A policy judges only the requests it is sent
A clean CLI run says nothing about whether the policy will see anything. In a cluster, it runs only if:
- there is a request at all;
- the API server, receiving every request, sends it to Kyverno;
- Kyverno answers in time.

== The lab: no container may run as root
section: 2. The lab: five Pods and a Deployment
Kubernetes enforces the rule when a container's securityContext.runAsNonRoot is true; one that does not set it inherits the Pod's. This is inherits, one of five Pods in step 2's pods.yaml. It and web comply; root, unset and init do not.
```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata: {name: inherits, namespace: shop}
spec:
  securityContext: {runAsNonRoot: true}
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: ghcr.io/acme/web:2.1.0
```

== Step 3: a ValidatingPolicy checks requests
section: 3. Your first ValidatingPolicy
This ends policies/run-as-non-root.yaml, step 3's ValidatingPolicy. matchConstraints picks requests; each validations expression must be true. orValue(false) supplies false when the field is missing.
```yaml
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: [v1]
        operations: [CREATE, UPDATE]
        resources: [pods]
  validations:
    - expression: >-
        object.spec.containers.all(c,
          c.?securityContext.?runAsNonRoot.orValue(false) == true)
      message: every container must set securityContext.runAsNonRoot to true
```

== Step 4: the container's value, else the Pod's
section: 4. The field that is not there
Step 3 wrongly failed inherits and passed init, whose init container runs first and may run as root. Step 4 replaces policies/run-as-non-root.yaml to check every kind of container; this part uses each one's value, else the Pod's, else fails.
```yaml
    - name: podDefault
      expression: object.spec.?securityContext.?runAsNonRoot.orValue(false)
    - name: offenders
      expression: >-
        variables.containers.filter(c,
          c.?securityContext.?runAsNonRoot.orValue(variables.podDefault) != true)
  validations:
    - expression: size(variables.offenders) == 0
```

== The match that never matches
section: 5. The match that never matches
matchConstraints needs the plural, lowercase resource name, pods. This sed line writes a copy that says Pods. The CLI reports no verdicts, and echo $? prints 0, the exit status for success: a pipeline checking only that goes green.
```bash
sed 's/resources: \[pods\]/resources: [Pods]/' policies/run-as-non-root.yaml > typo.yaml
kyverno apply typo.yaml --resource pods.yaml
echo $?
```

== Autogen: one Pod policy, every Pod controller
section: 6. Autogen: one Pod policy, every Pod controller
Step 4's policy judged deploy.yaml, step 2's Deployment: autogen gives a Pod policy variants for Deployments and other Pod controllers. This part of step 6's ephemeral.yaml adds a second rule, which switches autogen off: the Deployment gets no verdict.
```yaml
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: [v1]
        operations: [CREATE, UPDATE]
        resources: [pods]
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: [v1]
        operations: [UPDATE]
        resources: [pods/ephemeralcontainers]
```

== The CLI cannot tell Deny from Audit
section: 7. Deny, Audit, Warn, and failing open
The validationActions field says what a failed check does in a cluster: Deny rejects the request; Audit admits it and reports the failure; Warn admits it with a warning. This Audit copy prints exactly what step 4's Deny version prints.
```bash
sed 's/validationActions: \[Deny\]/validationActions: [Audit]/' policies/run-as-non-root.yaml > audit.yaml
kyverno apply audit.yaml --resource pods.yaml
echo $?
```

== Existing resources are only reported
section: 8. Existing resources, background scans and reports
A new denying policy judges requests, so nothing already running changes. The background scan re-checks matching resources hourly by default and records results in reports, but never blocks or deletes anything. The CLI's --policy-report flag prints such a report.
```bash
kyverno apply policies/run-as-non-root.yaml --resource pods.yaml --policy-report
```

== Tests say what each policy must find
section: 11. Test policies in CI
In CI (continuous integration), the kyverno test command compares each verdict with what you expected and catches a dead match. This is part of step 11's tests/run-as-non-root/kyverno-test.yaml; make its folder. A test that cannot fail proves nothing.
```yaml
  - policy: run-as-non-root
    isValidatingPolicy: true
    kind: Pod
    resources: [web, inherits]
    result: pass
  - policy: run-as-non-root
    isValidatingPolicy: true
    kind: Pod
    resources: [root, unset, init]
    result: fail
```

== Reading a legacy ClusterPolicy
section: 12. Reading a legacy ClusterPolicy
Step 12's legacy.yaml is a kyverno.io/v1 ClusterPolicy; 1.19 is the last release that runs ClusterPolicies. This part's =() anchor means: if the field is present, it must match, so unset passes. With no failure action, it defaults to Audit.
```yaml
      validate:
        message: every container must set securityContext.runAsNonRoot to true
        pattern:
          spec:
            containers:
              - =(securityContext):
                  =(runAsNonRoot): true
```

== Exercise: a replica cap that never fails
section: Hands-on exercises
Exercise 1's replicas.yaml, ending here, is meant to cap Deployments at 10 replicas. Run it against deploy.yaml and a copy named big with 50 replicas. Why does nothing fail? The guide hides each answer until you open Show the answer.
```yaml
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: [v1]
        operations: [CREATE, UPDATE]
        resources: [Deployments]
  validations:
    - expression: object.spec.replicas <= 10
      message: at most 10 replicas
```

== Answer: wrong group, wrong resource name
section: Hands-on exercises
Deployments live in the apps API group, not the core group "", and the resource name is deployments. Either mistake alone makes the policy match nothing. This part of replicas-fixed.yaml also reads replicas optionally: a manifest may leave it out.
```yaml
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [apps]
        apiVersions: [v1]
        operations: [CREATE, UPDATE]
        resources: [deployments]
  validations:
    - expression: object.spec.?replicas.orValue(1) <= 10
      message: at most 10 replicas
```

== Exercise: reject Pods running a latest image
section: Hands-on exercises
Write a policy that rejects Pods running a latest image. Exercise 2's tags.yaml holds four Pods: pinned uses ghcr.io/acme/web:2.1.0, latest uses nginx:latest, untagged uses nginx, and local uses registry.local:5000/tools. Try !c.image.endsWith(':latest') first: which Pods does it let through?

== Answer: parse the image reference
section: Hands-on exercises
The string check catches only the Pod named latest. An image with no tag means latest too, so untagged and local get through. Kyverno's CEL image library parses the reference; this is part of policies/no-latest.yaml.
```yaml
  validations:
    - expression: >-
        object.spec.containers.all(c, image(c.image).tag() != 'latest')
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- A policy that matches nothing exits 0; give each a kyverno test file.
- Write validationActions in every policy: legacy rules default to Audit, the current types to Deny.
- With failurePolicy: Ignore, requests go through unchecked whenever Kyverno is down.
